{
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "heading", "text": "What is Quantum Data Encoding?", "level": 2 },
    { "type": "text", "body": "Before a quantum computer can process classical data (images, signals, tables), the data must be mapped into quantum statevectors. This process is called **Quantum Data Encoding** or constructing a **Quantum Feature Map**." },
    { "type": "callout", "variant": "info", "body": "Quantum Feature Maps elevate 2D or N-D classical vectors into a high-dimensional Hilbert space (2^n dimensions), where linear hyperplanes can separate non-linearly separable data." },

    { "type": "heading", "text": "Encoding Strategies", "level": 2 },
    { "type": "text", "body": "1. **Basis Encoding:** Maps binary strings directly to computational basis states |x⟩." },
    { "type": "text", "body": "2. **Angle Encoding:** Rotates single qubits by rotation angles proportional to feature values: Rx(π x_0), Ry(π x_1)." },
    { "type": "text", "body": "3. **Amplitude Encoding:** Encodes 2^n feature values as amplitudes of an n-qubit statevector. Enables exponential compression!" },

    { "type": "heading", "text": "Angle Encoding Circuit Example", "level": 2 },
    { "type": "step_circuit", "numQubits": 2, "caption": "2-Qubit Angle Encoding Feature Map for 2D Point (x_0, x_1)",
      "gates": [
        { "id": "enc1", "gate": "RX", "qubits": [0], "column": 0, "param": 1.57 },
        { "id": "enc2", "gate": "RY", "qubits": [1], "column": 0, "param": 0.785 },
        { "id": "enc3", "gate": "CZ", "qubits": [0, 1], "column": 1 }
      ],
      "stepDescriptions": [
        "Initial state: |00⟩",
        "Encode feature x_0 into qubit 0 via Rx(π x_0) rotation",
        "Encode feature x_1 into qubit 1 via Ry(π x_1) rotation",
        "Entangle qubits with CZ to capture non-linear feature correlations"
      ]
    },

